Plugin 0012 (gpu/plugin0012, LT-B3; private analysis 20261124-browser-gpu section 14.7): a Metal client that may write in none of the
plugin's m2v cache-directory candidates still gets its device, without the translated-shader cache on disk.

Chrome's GPU helper (gpu.sb allow-metal-shader-cache) may create only Apple's Metal cache names under the user cache directory, so all
five `.probe-<pid>` creates were denied, native_bridge_metal_open was never called and the helper logged `no device: ` with an empty
reason; ANGLE then had no Metal device. The prebuilt bridge (libnative_metal_bridge.a 81d46fae) refuses a NULL cache path
(nmb_device_open: "loader and cache are required") but carries the runtime's METAL2VULKAN_SHADER_CACHE_OPTIONAL (UniXNU-turnip
AdrenoMetal bridge patch 0007, shader_cache/store.rs Directory::open): with it set, a directory that cannot be made or written opens
anyway and every disk read/write is a miss; the in-memory pipeline memo stays. UniXNU-turnip's own plugin (clientDevice) does the same
for WebKit's GPU process.

Only when no candidate took the probe: a process with a writable directory (WindowServer, Safari's GPU process, apps) keeps its disk
cache exactly as before, and one whose open was refused in a writable directory is not opened again. The variable is cleared after the
open (it is read only at device creation).

Applied by tooling/metal/build-turnip-a830.sh to $PIANO_K27_TREE/overlay/metal/NativeAdrenoMetalPlugin.mm (sha256 6d59c5f2).
